plan EntityDomains
title   Entity domains: coherent records, conservative repair and honest predictions
status  proposed
goal
    Give plain operations a declared shared-data meaning without author-written cache maintenance.
    One Runtime store keeps record fields coherent across domains. Machines own workflow; the
    domain records places, dependencies, predictions and server effects. Repair is conservative,
    bounded and explainable, and never predicts list membership.

    This Plan replaces FlowState O6 and owns references/evidence/ as design evidence to port,
    not shipped implementation. references/design/entity-domain.md sections 1-9 are adopted in
    full subject to the explicit amendments here and the binding split-work decisions. Its
    examples and rationale explain the rules; neither archived Plans nor example code supply
    unstated current requirements. references/ledger/EntityDomains.md records the transfer.
outcomes
    O1  The normative design and its amendments
        An operation describes a request; a domain describes its meaning for shared data
        (references/design/entity-domain.md sections 1-4). Operations own input, plain result
        Schema, adapter, key, freshness and actor concurrency; operation modules never import
        entities. Adopting entities edits the domain, not the operations. domain(id, entries)
        lists read(resource), write(transaction) and listen(stream) entries. An operation is
        registered in at most one domain and machines reach it only through O.Domain.entry.
        Machines subscribe to read and listen entries as activities. Domains never send, commit,
        read another resource or choose a state; runtime callbacks compute only declared values.
        Entities belong to no domain: every domain may hold, predict or target an entity, and
        the repair join spans every active answer in the App. A team declares its own writes'
        extra effects with alsoChanges, never enumerates another team's writes.

        entity({ id, fields, primaryKey, version? }) declares Schema fields and key fields; a
        nested entity field holds that entity wherever the parent is held. Version metadata is
        excluded from condition/order record proxies and patches. One entity object per id is
        allowed in the adopted graph; duplicated module copies are collisions. At most one
        byKey loader per entity exists across the adopted App. result Schema is required for
        domain reads, so places and carried field shapes can be checked (@plan.FlowOperations.O1).
        flow check errors on duplicate byKey loaders and operations registered in two domains
        or reached outside their registering domain; types reject registered-value misuse where
        visible. An unregistered operation whose result carries an entity's key fields produces
        a flow check warning, rather than silently acquiring domain behavior.

        Places and dependencies:

        - one(Entity, at?) holds one record, the whole result by default; list(Entity, at,
        member?) holds records along a recorded path. An array on the path means each element.
        Shape inference requires every key and checks every same-named entity field's type;
        other fields remain result context. Entity.pick names an explicit shape and rejects
        other same-named fields, avoiding accidental live-field interpretation of historical
        nested data. Different entities' same-named fields never collide. A singleton gets its
        key from its response. Several list places may share an entry; overlapping key names
        require a tagged union and explicit member tags. Its record proxy exposes shared fields
        including the tag. Nested paths are typed; a nested record change reaches parent answers
        only through declared paths, or a server-ranked list not unaffected by that field.
        - byKey(({ input }) => key) declares the record loader. derivedFrom(Entity, fields?,
        where?) declares values computed from records the response does not hold. Omitted fields
        means any field; an empty field list means membership only. Condition fields always
        affect the computation. Multiple derived bindings each take their own third-argument
        condition; an entry-level where on several derivedFrom bindings is a type error.
        - where uses eq, oneOf, contains, between, overlaps and and; repeated where calls combine
        with and. Arguments are typed record/input fields, principal() or constants. between
        and overlaps use half-open intervals. An absent optional input removes its constraint;
        absent range endpoints leave the corresponding side unbounded. Different absence
        semantics require serverMatches. serverMatches(input, ...fields) may match any record;
        its named fields are condition fields, or all fields when none are named. Every list or
        derived input field must occur in where, orderBy, pagedBy, ignoring or serverMatches;
        unused input is a type error at domain construction, with serverMatches offered before
        ignoring. ignoring is only a claim that an input affects neither membership nor order;
        explain prints it separately. Constant filters and extra server effects must be declared;
        static checks cannot discover an omitted filter with no input.
        - orderBy records asc/desc keys and ends with every entity key, with a union tag before
        the keys. Any direction is legal. A list has one ordering choice: orderBy, unordered or
        the default server-ranked order, optionally narrowed by unaffectedBy. unaffectedBy is
        limited to carried non-key fields of a server-ranked list, not an ordered list. Orders
        are server claims, never client sorting. On every response, check that response's own
        numeric, boolean, date and key values where comparable; strings are not compared because
        server collation is unknown. HiddenOrder names the conflicting records and first key;
        demote that answer to server-ranked for the Runtime's remaining lifetime and record it.
        Missing tie keys are detectable only when records tie on preceding checked keys.
        unordered is unchecked and appropriate only when the view does not rely on response order.
        - pagedBy(inputSelector, nextSelector) names the page input and next input of that same
        type, absent at the end. Inputs without the paging field identify one paged answer.
        O.Domain.entry.more(select) loads after its last page; the lane exposes hasMore, never
        more. Repair rebuilds from page one, using each fresh response's continuation, up to the
        previously loaded count or the new end. It publishes the whole chain at once, serving
        the previous chain stale until then. Failure preserves the old chain stale with failure;
        an affecting delta mid-rebuild fences it and rebuilds from page one. Extra list metadata
        beyond records and cursor has no repair guarantee (decision 36): flow check warns;
        model totals and aggregates as derived reads. This overrides minimal-library's deferred
        infinite-list scope; plain resources do not gain paging.

        Recording (design section 4): places, byKey, where, orderBy, pagedBy, ignoring,
        derivedFrom fields/conditions, alsoChanges targets, Entity.where and predicts' entity,
        key and field names are recorded once at domain construction with proxies into plain
        ref/val/func expression data. predicts runs again at write start with real input;
        function-valued patches receive the record. as runs for every real stream event and is
        opaque except for its declared entities. Tool output distinguishes opaque runtime
        computation from fully recorded structure. Recording is fingerprinted into App identity.

        A recorded position is one expression of property reads and library operators. Boolean
        comparisons fail construction with the correct operator form (write eq instead of ===);
        arithmetic, templates, coercion and method calls fail with the recorded place. Types and
        proxies cannot detect a conditional or short-circuit that records only one branch:
        mandatory flow check errors reject ?:, if, &&, ||, impure calls (including Date.now and
        Math.random), outer mutable state and unreadable compiled/generated declarations. The
        source check also runs at development construction. Runtime field functions may compute
        ordinary arithmetic; unknown server logic belongs in serverMatches.

        Changes and targets:

        - predicts returns a change or array: Entity.patch(key or keys, fields), or Entity.delete.
        A patch may affect only an entity named in a place or alsoChanges target of that entry;
        a delete may name any entity. Keys may be arrays; field functions are typed against the
        record and real input; version patches are type errors. A write may hold several one
        places, list places or no result place at all.
        - alsoChanges returns a target or array. Entity.all reaches every answer on that entity;
        Entity.where reaches answers whose conditions could overlap (contradictory equalities
        on one field exclude, other combinations conservatively overlap); Entity.fields(key,
        fields) marks those fields unconfirmed, schedules confirmation, and repairs lists only
        where those fields affect conditions or order. A target to an entity with no holders
        costs no work now and remains in the declaration and trace for future reads.
        - listen(stream).as([Entities], mapping) may produce changes only to those listed
        entities: types reject others and runtime validation names an offending event. Ordered
        stream delivery is a backend precondition. Domain operations forbid call-site optimistic,
        setData and clear; writes may contain only invalidate (was FlowState D37).

        Store and repair (design section 5): one record per entity key holds received fields;
        each field has its own version, source start position and landed position. Base means
        server-confirmed; visible means base plus live predictions replayed in write-start order
        whenever the base changes. Versions are per field: an omitted field's version does not
        advance. A source wins if both versions exist and it is strictly greater; lower loses,
        equal falls through. Otherwise its start must be at or after the stored field's landed
        position. Lookups start at adapter invocation, pushes on arrival, confirmed write fields
        at settlement. Any response fills an absent field even when older than another field.
        Without versions, a differing field is contested when it lands on a source set after
        it started or is rejected by ordering. Refetch its loader and all holders; a contest
        revealed by a repair response only marks stale. Record the contest; never invent a merge.

        Predictions affect visibility only: never lower frontiers, fence lookups or start repairs.
        An answer is pending while it holds a live prediction. Replay functions over each new
        base; two increments remain two increments, removing one leaves the other. A later
        confirmed write field retires every older prediction of that field on that record.

        The join matches base deltas against active answers, on old OR new values. A missing
        condition field conservatively matches. An unheld record has no old side for lists,
        since active lists hold their records; derived reads treat its old value as unknown and
        matching. First sightings therefore repair derived answers once. The repair classifier:

        - one repairs on deletion; field copies otherwise stay coherent through the store.
        - ordered lists repair on entering/leaving or an in-condition order-field change;
        unordered lists repair only on entering/leaving; server-ranked lists also repair on any
        field change except fields declared unaffectedBy.
        - derived answers repair on entering/leaving or a relevant field changing on a matching
        record. Omitted fields means all, empty fields means membership only. Condition fields
        always matter. Merely displaying a field never requires repair.
        - alsoChanges, invalidate, reconnect and restore affect every covered answer, irrespective
        of a visible field delta; hydrate adds the source defined in @O3.

        A behind response (lower version than the store for a record) makes its own answer's
        membership stale: a machine request refetches, a repair response only marks stale. A
        response or push rejected by ordering or tombstone still joins derived answers, from its
        values to the current base: the derived answer may reflect that intermediate server
        state. Rejected values from repair responses also stop at stale marking.

        A settled write repairs the union of two deltas: base before prediction to response,
        and base at settlement to response. An intervening push's effects cannot be lost.
        Machine-request observations (including staleTime and focus refetches) repair normally.
        A repair response updates copies and marks affected answers stale with the observation
        cause, but starts NO lookup, including contested, rejected, behind and confirming cases.
        The next machine request or local cause refreshes them. The fence works in both
        directions: new deltas match old answers, and new answers match in-flight deltas;
        a request started before an affecting change is stale on arrival.

        Each answer has at most one repair in flight and one trailing refetch. Matching deltas
        fence the current one and coalesce into the trailing one. Indexes may optimise a scan
        (equality, oneOf, contains, ranges), but must agree with its result. Each answer frontier
        accounts for changes through a comparable opaque position. A newer matching delta makes
        it stale; a request started at or after the delta may advance it. Lower-version fields
        advance no frontier and confirm no prediction. Read-your-writes is a backend precondition:
        reads started after settlement must see it; older replicas are unsupported. Reconnect,
        restore and invalidate lower covered frontiers; subscribed answers refetch in canonical
        order under @O5, unsubscribed answers only become stale.

        Consolidate base deltas per record per scheduler turn before joining, in Stories and
        production alike. Consolidate values, never causes: even a net-zero change retains
        obligations for server intermediate effects. Obligations are keyed by answer and cause;
        one lookup may satisfy several and records them all. The closed repair causes are write
        settlement (success, failure, uncertain), push, machine-request observation, alsoChanges,
        invalidate, reconnect, restore, contested field, confirming read, and hydrate (@O3).

        Write endings and confirmation:

        - Success installs response fields and ends confirmed predictions in ONE publication;
        omitted predicted fields stay unconfirmed and pending. Repair both bases and schedule
        confirming reads. Failure or defect removes predictions and repairs affected answers
        for every predicted field, because a failed server call may have partly applied.
        Rejection or cancellation before invocation removes predictions without repair.
        - Uncertain means cancelled after invocation, response lost or restored mid-flight.
        Force the loader and every holder to refetch, keep predictions until forced repair lands,
        then show server state now. Keep uncertain until a later successful write or explicit
        attempt reconciliation. A read cannot prove the lost request will never apply later.
        - A confirming read starts after settlement, carries that record's field, and passes
        ordering; it ends the prediction even for an equal value. Choose deterministically:
        first an already-refetching affected answer carrying the field, then the loader, then
        the first active carrying answer, all in canonical order. Omitting the record confirms
        nothing; remove it only from that answer and try the next candidate. Loader notFound
        confirms a predicted delete, except never delete an unsettled create. With no active
        carrier end the prediction and drop that field from base so the next reader fetches.
        Failure leaves it pending and unconfirmed until a later accepted carrying read.
        - Until confirmation, show the prediction without flicker. Applying a response and
        ending its predictions is one publication: never expose a value older than a settled
        write's confirmed field. Repair keeps the previous complete answer stale until replaced.

        Predicting writes add a cross-actor FIFO queue per record beneath actor transaction
        concurrency (amends archived Transactions D1). Actor reject/allow/serialize decides
        occurrence start first; predictions apply at that start (serialize at dequeue).
        concurrency cancel on predicting writes is a flow check error. Join all predicted record
        queues atomically with one common order; invoke only when head of all, so overlapping
        multi-record writes cannot deadlock. Show predictions while queued. Explicit cancellation
        of a queued, never-invoked occurrence removes it and settles interrupted without adapter
        work. Success/failure/rejection releases the next; uncertain releases only after forced
        repair lands, replaying later predictions over repaired base. No connectivity/focus gate.

        unknown delivers the exported Attempt Schema (id and encoded input, with operation/key
        identity under @plan.FlowOperations.O3); machines author an uncertainty state with an exit.
        O.X.retry(select, options) reissues that exact Attempt and id; a known settled attempt
        settles immediately without invocation (decisions 6 and 36). basedOn gives the version
        each prediction was computed over for an API's If-Match; it cannot prove the old write
        finished. Persist predicted keys with uncertain attempts; persisted actors acknowledge
        the Attempt write before adapter invocation. Non-persisted actors have no crash durability.

        Creates return the record; their nothing-to-record delta repairs matching answers.
        Membership appears only when those answers refetch. The composer can show its draft
        beside the answer until the key appears; there are no temporary ids or optimistic inserts.
        A predicted move changes fields immediately but keeps old list membership until repair;
        an instant board move uses a parent unordered list grouped/sorted in the view. Predicted
        delete hides the record everywhere with pending; failure restores it; uncertainty shows
        forced-repair truth. Confirmed delete hides everywhere and leaves a positional tombstone
        until all pre-delete lookups end. A versioned delete's tombstone lasts to record gcTime;
        saves at lower or equal versions cannot resurrect it. Unversioned pushes order by arrival;
        the positional guard covers lookups only, and flow check warns on unversioned listen.
        Non-list references read a typed Deleted value and make holders stale.

        Hold records while an answer references them (through its gcTime after last subscriber),
        a loader subscription holds them or a prediction is live. Persist only confirmed data:
        persisted answer references/context and only record fields those answers project.
        Restore at position 0 and lower frontiers so live truth wins; principal change creates
        a new Runtime. No prediction is persisted. Answer reads are resource lanes plus stale:
        false or its causes; failed repair retains data as failed refresh. Record reads are
        { data, pending, uncertain, unconfirmed }, with unconfirmed listing field names.

        value outcomes see confirmed data only (decision 12): changes compare with Equal.equals;
        predictions and optimistic layers never fire value, equal values are silent. A machine
        memory baseline therefore never captures a prediction (@plan.FlowOperations.O2).
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/ticket.domain.ts,
        features/Tickets/queue/queue.test.ts and features/Tickets/editor/editor.machine.ts.
    O2  Rules restated from archived citations
        The following imports are stated here so no archived citation has residual authority.
        Current capability owners supply the full contracts at @plan.Testing.O1,
        @plan.Testing.O2, @plan.Testing.O3, @plan.Testing.O4, @plan.Inspection.O1,
        @plan.Inspection.O3, @plan.Inspection.O4 and @plan.FlowCommandLine.O4.

        Archived StoriesAndModels O1-O3 (imported by design section 6): builders are inert,
        commands create persistent plans, branches replay independently, each run owns one fresh
        production Runtime, deterministic Runtime Clock and seeded ids. No domain-specific Story
        kind or command is added. App Stories use ensure(ref, options) and send(ref, event);
        focused Stories use send(event) and setContext(context). Both support process, advance,
        advanceTo, advanceToNextTimer, checkpoint and run({ signal? }); explicit dehydrate/hydrate
        commands are the server-rendering amendment (@plan.Testing.O7). Advance moves time but
        does not process. process drains ready mailbox/context/reconciliation/deadline/scheduler
        and finite work without moving time or inventing results; its private 10,000-turn budget
        fails with StoryTurnLimit and evidence, never a public maxTurns option (decision 1).

        Fixtures replace service behavior through ordinary complete Effect Layers, never kernels,
        pending-occurrence mocks, arbitrary cache mutation or seeds. Fresh Ref state is allocated
        inside each fixture Layer scope per run; pushes use the stream fixture and Runtime Clock.
        Layer.provideMerge(fixtures, setup.services) lets fixtures require setup services and
        shadow their tags; duplicate fixture tags reject unless replacement is explicitly marked
        overriding; provide-nothing fixtures are allowed (decisions 7 and 37). Focused Stories
        take services for service closure. Every Story uses fresh in-memory KeyValueStore,
        never the application's external storage. Domains generate no API fixture.

        Cancellation closes admission, stops later commands, preserves partial evidence and waits
        for finalization. Close sinks/queues, dispose actors in reverse dependency order and
        Runtime owners, then fixture/host scopes. Execution or cancellation failure stays primary;
        otherwise first cleanup failure is primary; cleanup failure never returns success.
        FlowError preserves full Cause through cleanup, completed checkpoints, end if captured,
        failing command index, ordered cleanup diagnostics, cancelled and accepted/drained
        sequence facts. Checkpoints are evidence, never resumable Runtimes.

        Archived StoriesAndModels O4/V4 (design sections 6.2, 6.3 and 6.5): checkpoint captures
        immediately without a drain; run.end follows commands before cleanup and is not actor
        finality. Capture one atomic cut of published snapshots, one store revision, pendingWork,
        Runtime time, issues and accepted evidence prefix. Current membership is every live actor
        in the fresh Story Runtime, replacing old recipe closure. Suspended context debt fails
        retryably as ConcurrentCapture; no half-published cut. Copy Flow-owned envelopes, records,
        arrays, readers, keys, issues and snapshot containers; opaque application values retain
        identity. Reads do not touch live state, advance time or start work. Failed capture never
        fabricates end; completed checkpoints survive later failure. Untrusted checkpoint names
        require own-key membership; inherited/unknown names reject as FlowError.

        Entity.get(checkpoint, key, shape) reads exactly that cut: data/status for requested fields,
        missing fields explicitly missing, undefined if the record is absent. Domain.entry.get
        returns the lane plus stale, pages in order, or undefined if no active answer exists
        (never subscribed or collected). Entity.queue returns ordered attempts, writes and queue
        states. Entity.get(snapshot, key, shape) through useView is passive via private Runtime
        bookkeeping; CheckpointProvider reads its checkpoint. No public store revision is added
        (archived SurfaceSimplification D22). Every checkpoint and run.end carries repairs,
        skips and diagnostics projected from its accepted trace prefix, including contests,
        HiddenOrder and selfCheck. repairs record sequence, read/input, causes, optional record,
        changed fields, match entered/left/both/target and action refetch/trailing/joined/observed.
        skips record outside-condition/unaffected/fresh for considered candidates only. Inactive
        answers appear in neither. Order is delta, domain entry position, canonical input key.
        In-process checkpoint/live values are faithful, including sensitive fields (decision 4
        supersedes design sections 6.3/7.4); exported evidence redacts sensitive values as keyed
        HMAC digests with a per-Runtime, per-run in Stories, key.

        Archived InspectionAndArtifacts O3 (design sections 5.6 and 6.3): evidence is one
        Runtime-global sequence shared with turns and lifecycle facts. Publication and evidence
        acceptance precede acknowledgment, which never waits for a user sink. Lifecycle snapshot
        precedes its event without manufacturing a machine turn. Capacity defaults to 256 and is
        an exact non-negative integer including zero. A snapshot includes records and
        truncatedBeforeSequence, null only when nothing accepted was omitted; otherwise it is
        the greatest omitted/cleared sequence. clear preserves sequence and advances only through
        retained tail; queued accepted records can arrive later. A sink belongs to one Runtime,
        sees post-attachment facts, and late attachment marks unseen history. Drain/dispose waits
        through accepted prefixes before detaching; a failing sink detaches alone and reports
        failure. No silent omissions, mixed-Runtime history or persisted buffers. Reserve cleanup
        and evidence safe-integer headroom before mutation; sequence exhaustion refuses commit.
        If a question's required prefix is truncated, answer insufficient evidence, never none.

        Archived SurfaceSimplification O2, D39 and D40 (design section 7): recorded structure is
        one inert internal representation used by graph, check, explain and renderers, not a
        second model or saved behavior artifact. inspect(app) is structural (now async to load
        every lazy entry, @plan.LazyFeatures.O5); inspect(runtime) is a passive revision-consistent
        live cut, starts no work and never adopts. Domain descriptions include operation ids,
        entries, place paths, expression trees, orders, paging, ignored inputs, unaffectedBy,
        predicted entities/fields, targets, as entity lists and indexed/scanned status. Bound
        recorded literals with the shared structural codec (@plan.Inspection.O2). render text
        prints the declaration sentences; render json is the agent form, both golden-tested.

        Static flow explain domain runs no adapters: its impact table uses the SAME runtime
        classifier and covers every observed generated repair. --read domain.entry reverses the
        view across domains and includes subscribing machines from graph. Evidence mode reads
        trace artifacts without application execution: explain --trace --at explains activity,
        effective/demoted rules and joins; trace summarize --read or --entity (exclusive), with
        optional --sequence, explains repairs, field provenance/rejected contenders, confirming
        reads, queue heads/halts and uncertainty; trace diff --section repairs compares normalised
        ordered decisions. Unknown domain/read/entity/key/artifact/checkpoint exits 2; producing
        an explanation, stale or insufficient evidence included, exits 0. A truncated absence
        cannot establish an unknown selector (@plan.FlowCommandLine.O4).

        Archived InspectionAndArtifacts O3/O4, amended by design section 7.4: emit ordered delta
        (source, fields, digests, as event digest and mapping result), field (accept/reject/contest,
        compared versions/positions), repair, skip, lookup (causes, outcome, pages), prediction
        (start/replay/confirm/retire/end, attempt, fields, chosen read), queue (join/send/halt/
        release/remove), uncertain (open/forced repair landed/end), answer (activate/deactivate/
        demote), invalidate/reconnect/restore/hydrate (coverage), tombstone/gc (record, reason),
        selfCheck and audit facts. Every event has the Runtime-global sequence. Every turn counts
        deltas, candidates, repairs, skips, lookups and pages; summarize totals them per source.
        Artifacts carry field names and digests, not arbitrary historical values. Inspector sinks
        use the same events without delaying turns and apply export redaction (decision 4).
        Live domain inspection exposes answers/frontiers/stale causes/effective rules, predictions,
        queues and uncertainty at one store revision; late observers receive the current cut and
        truncation marker, never invented history.

        Archived Transactions D1 (design section 5.5) is amended explicitly by @O1's cross-actor
        predicting-record queues. Its actor-level concurrency remains first; queue/fiber topology
        stays private; one settlement and fencing still bind. Archived CLI/inspection/Story
        amendment tables in design sections 6.5/7.6 are implemented through the current owners
        cited above. No archived command, fixture constructor, public revision, actor-local
        prediction or unlimited history is restored merely by those tables.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/queue/queue.story.ts,
        features/Tickets/queue/queue.test.ts, tools/inspection.ts and tools/cli.ts.
    O3  Peer and hydrate sources
        Hydration is in version 1 (decisions 30 and 33), overriding entity-domain section 1.2's
        exclusion. references/design/server-rendering.md sections 2.7-2.8 and 3 amend the store:
        hydrate joins the closed cause list and lowers covered frontiers like restore. Transfer
        confirmed fields with versions; positions and frontiers remain local to each Runtime.
        Never transfer predictions, record queues, tombstones or uncertain marks. A hydrated
        byKey record is a normal holder for subsequent confirming reads.

        Absent fields are filled. For present versioned fields greater version wins, lower loses,
        equal falls through to the unversioned rule. For unversioned/equal-version present fields
        keep client data; differing payload data contests the field and refetches its loader and
        holders under the normal one-hop rule. Never compare request positions across Runtimes.
        Existing answer references win; different payload references mark it stale with hydrate.
        Absent answers insert fresh unless that entity has accepted any base delta, prediction,
        uncertain mark or queued write since ready(), in which case mark stale with hydrate.
        Existing predictions remain and replay over accepted bases. On initial bootstrap,
        persisted actors beat hydrated actors, hydrated data beats position-0 restored data,
        and strictly newer versioned persisted fields still win. Activation uses silent binding
        and confirmed-value baselines, so hydration never manufactures an initial value event.
        Payload selection omits sensitive fields/items as @plan.ServerRendering.O3 requires
        (decision 30 supersedes decision 4's SSR exception); no digest is hydrated as a field value.

        Peer amendment is DEFERRED, not a v1 source. references/design/multi-tab.md section 8
        proposes versioned confirmed peer deltas, unversioned invalidations, one-hop propagation,
        and no predictions/queues, but decision 38 supersedes decisions 32/33's multi-tab adoption.
        v1 has no MultiTab plan, leader, mirror, BroadcastChannel, peer cause, peer record writes
        or two-tab entity property mode. Persistence instead requests an ifAvailable Web Lock
        for (app id, scope) at ready(), holds it to dispose, and on denial runs without restore
        or capture with SecondTabNoPersistence. With no navigator.locks, persistence proceeds
        without a check. @plan.FlowPersistence.O1 owns this boundary. Atomic scope writes still bind.

        Lazy domain joining (decision 31; references/design/lazy-features.md section 5) joins the
        same Runtime store and repair graph synchronously at admission before ensure/create.
        Record every reached entity, operation and domain, close transitive provider wiring,
        then check ids, one object per entity id, one domain per operation and one byKey per
        entity across adopted and incoming graphs. Fail with existing construction diagnostic
        plus details.entry. Decode dormant records detached, install at position 0 with lowered
        frontiers, and publish one cut of structure and restored truth before actor admission
        continues. Adoption never occurs inside a macrostep; no turn sees a partial graph.
        Failure rolls back all incoming graph/data changes; entry stays inactive and retries
        from the beginning. Passive reads/capture/live inspection do not adopt. A loader in an
        inactive entry is unavailable, so no-carrier confirmation drops the field safely; flow
        check warns and recommends the loader be eager or colocated with entity users.
        @plan.LazyFeatures.O2 and @plan.LazyFeatures.O3 own adoption and dormant records.
        Reference: none yet (stage S10 adds it).
    O4  propertyTest and audit mode
        propertyTest(domains, { fixture, inputs, seed, runs, steps }) is a flow-state/testing
        host helper, never a new Story kind. It generates ordinary static Stories with fresh
        production Runtimes; explicit domains suffice without an App. Probe actors subscribe to
        every read using explicit read-input generators; overlapping entities' domains are tested
        together. It generates writes (success/failure/uncertain), reordered/duplicated/failed/
        empty reads, pushes, reconnect, restore, unsubscribe/resubscribe and invalidate, plus
        hydrate from model Runtime A into B and adopt-domain-now steps. The helper owns generated
        scheduling through fixtures, never replaces the runtime algorithm. @plan.Testing.O6 owns
        generator representation, fixture-oracle access and replayable shrinking.

        The fixture oracle reads its own server state, not the domain's conditions, orders or
        repair classifier. A fixture copied from the domain proves only self-consistency; real
        API contract tests are needed for backend fidelity. Flow's implementation suite uses an
        independent evaluator and known-bad domain variants; app authors need no mandatory test
        merely to use a small domain. Observe EVERY committed publication through production
        evidence, even when process drains several turns. Check C1-C8 from entities.md:

        - C1 coherence at every publication: all copies of each field agree. C2 convergence at
        quiet: active answers equal oracle element-by-element in declared order, or are stale
        with observed causes and agree after one machine request, with uncertainty explicit.
        - C3 no cascades: no repair-response lookup, every lookup has causes, repairs per cause
        respect bounds. C4 monotonic truth: no accepted field regresses by version or position.
        - C5 honest prediction: every displayed unconfirmed value is a live pending prediction;
        each unconfirmed field has a scheduled or failed confirming read; no old field flicker
        after confirmed settlement; no prediction persisted. C6 no silent loss: a record leaves an 
        answer only because the server deleted it,
        a response omitted it, or a live prediction hides it, with the reason recorded.
        - C7 determinism: replay failing plans and sampled passing plans twice with identical
        traces. C8 explainability: every repair, skip, hidden record and prediction has causes.

        Quiet is process, then advanceToNextTimer/process until no timer or a horizon, then
        checkpoint. Advance far afterwards; no new lookup may start. Outcomes are pass, fail or
        inconclusive (turn budget reached or external work pending), never convert inconclusive
        to success. Shrink failures into literal static Stories with seed and failed property;
        rerun before printing. Initial workload defaults 50 runs of 40 steps remain measurements
        to evaluate, not claims of sufficient coverage.

        Audit is an explicit Runtime development/test option, off by default and in Stories
        unless enabled, never on in production. After every settlement, push or observed change,
        refetch EVERY active answer not repaired and compare without publishing. HiddenFilter
        reports membership differences; UndeclaredEffect names a write, read and missing
        alsoChanges. It is exhaustive and deterministic with a fixture; its cost is one refetch
        per unrepaired active answer per change. Response selfChecks always test declared
        conditions/order; they cannot establish absent semantics outside audit/property tests.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/testing/tickets.property.test.ts and 
        testing/fixtures.ts.
    O5  Bounds
        Matching costs active answers scanned or index hits; indexes must agree with the scan.
        Per answer: one repair in flight and one trailing; Runtime repair concurrency is an
        option defaulting to 6, with starts in canonical order. Paged repair costs N sequential
        requests for N loaded pages; all count as one repair slot. Prediction replay costs live
        predictions bounded by finite per-record queues. Queue capacity is 64 waiting occurrences
        per record, aligned with @plan.FlowOperations.O3's serialize queue: reject the whole incoming
        multi-record admission before predictions, queue joins or adapter work if any queue
        would overflow, with AdmissionOverloaded naming the record and limit (decision 21).
        Canonical repair order is delta, authored domain entry order, canonical input key;
        cross-domain ties use stable domain id. Tombstones cost deletes within gcTime (plus
        outstanding pre-delete lookup guards). Recording occurs once per domain at construction.
        Evidence uses @O2's exact bounded retention and truncation; recorded constants and
        persisted/SSR/artifact data use @plan.Inspection.O2's structural limits: depth 32,
        10,000 nodes, 4,096 array items, 262,144-byte strings and 2 MiB canonical size. Operation
        keys retain their distinct 16-level, 256-node, 8,192-byte limits (@plan.FlowOperations.O1).
        High-frequency membership churn across many pages belongs to tier 2, not an exception
        to these bounds. Production has no cross-turn execution budget; Stories use @O2's bound.
        Reference: none yet (stage S10 adds it).
decisions
    D1  Shared-data policy is owned by domains
        Was FlowState D37. Predictions replace call-site optimistic and setData; clear is also
        forbidden and writes allows invalidate only. The full design sections 1-9 contract is
        written here with imports restated in @O2, so archived Plans remain provenance only.
    D2  Confirmed data alone feeds value outcomes
        Was FlowState D29 as applied to entities; binding decision 12. Equal.equals changes only,
        never predictions or layers, so saved memory cannot become an optimistic baseline.
    D3  Attempts reconcile explicitly and list metadata needs a declared read
        Decision 36: unknown carries Attempt, retry reuses it, known settlement never invokes
        again; arbitrary list metadata has no repair guarantee and produces a flow check warning.
    D4  Hydration and lazy joining amend the design; peers remain future work
        Decisions 30, 31, 33 and overriding 38. @O3 states the current merge and admission rules;
        multi-tab.md is a deferred design and grants no peer replication authority in v1.
    D5  Evidence is a porting input and mutations are obligations
        Decision 27: references/evidence/type-spike and runtime-model are retained with their
        commands. Their historical passes prove the design models only. The package needs all
        @V1-@V6 gates, including targeted tests for the four surviving runtime-model mutants.
    D6  Schema sensitivity controls exported evidence, not checkpoint truth
        Was FlowState D48, amended by decisions 4 and 30. sensitive fields are faithful in
        checkpoints and passive in-process reads; artifacts and inspector sinks use keyed HMAC
        digests. SSR payloads omit sensitive items instead of hydrating digest placeholders.
    D7  Activity lanes hold paged answers
        Was FlowState D39 and D54 as applied to domains. Snapshot lanes use Operations O6's tags;
        domain lanes add stale causes, and paged data holds ordered pages with hasMore per
        decision 14. Domain.entry.get returns that same revision-bound shape.
    D8  Per-record waiting capacity is finite and atomic
        Decision 21 requires a finite limit. Choose 64 waiting occurrences per predicted record
        to match Operations' serialize capacity, not an unbounded queue; @O5 defines admission
        rejection before any partial multi-record join. Measurement may justify a later change.
constraints
    C1  Tier 1 never invents server answers
        No predicted list membership, instant inserts, offline completeness, client-built query
        results, arbitrary predicate where, server-semantic inference, field conflict merge,
        automatic uncertain retry, rollback claim, partial-page repair or minimum-refetch promise.
        Machines own uncertain workflow. Tier-2 sync-engine integration is deferred.
    C2  Keep one production algorithm and current authority
        No domain execution engine, fixture generator, second explanation classifier, cache
        escape hatch, public mutable store or historical-value reconstruction from digests.
        Archived citations confer no additional surface. Plan checks do not prove implementation.
    C3  Preserve principal and revision isolation
        Separate Runtime per principal/request; no cross-Runtime positions/frontiers, live reads
        from checkpoints, persisted predictions or version regression. Faithful in-process reads
        and redacted exported evidence obey @O2; SSR omissions obey @O3.
verification
    V1  Real-package authoring and type gate
        Port references/evidence/type-spike against the real package and pinned toolchain:
        exact unannotated types for all eleven domains and sixteen equality assertions, plus
        deliberately false equality rejection. Cover every negative: non-total/double order,
        version in condition/order/patch, unaffectedBy wrong fields or order, unused list/derived
        input (including inline entry inference blocked from expected-return widening), wrong
        page continuation, unpaged more, union member/shared field mismatch, nested type,
        singleton/pick, undeclared patch/as entity, field-function return, key target shape,
        comparisons/conversions/method calls, call-site optimistic/setData/clear. Test source
        rejection of conditionals, short-circuiting, impurity and unreadable declarations; a
        successful type-only conditional must still fail the mandatory source check. Include
        actual predicts execution with real input. Planned, not established by the scratch model.
    V2  Repair and publication property gate
        Port runtime-model/model.mjs into the independent oracle suite on the real Runtime.
        Reproduce its 40,000 runs (20,000 seeds with and without omitted fields), C1-C8 at every
        publication/quiet point and eight caught-rule mutant families: both bases, behind answer,
        rejected sources, fence, unknown old values, one-hop, per-field versions, forced repair.
        Named model variants must fail when their rule is removed; a passing mutant blocks S5.
        Add unversioned contests, deletes, pages, nested entities, overlapping domains, scan/index
        parity, one-in-flight/trailing, net-zero values retaining causes, first sightings,
        invalidation, reconnection, persistence and audit absent from the old model. Planned.
    V3  Four surviving mutants have targeted tests
        Kill mut-no-confirming-read: a write returns a projection omitting its predicted field;
        a list lacking that field cannot confirm; the selected loader confirms at an equal value
        without flicker, a missing record tries the next carrier, failure stays pending, and no
        carrier drops the old base field. The test fails if predictions end by any read or never
        schedule the required confirming read.

        Kill mut-no-failure-repair: fixture partly applies a multi-field write then fails/defects;
        removing predictions alone must not satisfy the test. Assert both affected list/derived
        repairs and oracle agreement after repair, retaining per-cause evidence.

        Kill mut-no-retire-older: first write succeeds without carrying its predicted field and
        its confirming read is delayed; a later queued write confirms a newer value of the same
        field. Assert retirement of the older prediction in that publication, no replay over the
        newer truth and no resurrection when the delayed read arrives.

        Kill mut-no-tombstone-guard using an UNVERSIONED entity: begin a lookup before delete,
        confirm deletion, then deliver the older lookup. Assert no resurrection or lost delete
        evidence while the positional tombstone exists, then release the guard only after all
        pre-delete lookups end. A versioned tombstone alone cannot make this test pass. Planned.
    V4  Imported evidence, tools and honesty gate
        Exact checkpoint store-revision reads, copying and failed capture; raw sensitive fields
        in-process, keyed digests on export and no sensitive SSR values; canonical repairs/skips,
        truncation reporting insufficient evidence, capacities 0/256 and overflow, sequence
        exhaustion and late/failing sinks. Golden text/json descriptions and impact tables must
        agree with observed oracle repairs; test reverse-domain explain, trace filters/exits and
        passive revision-consistent inspect(runtime). Audit catches an omitted constant filter
        and undeclared side effect without publishing its reads; defaults stay off. Planned.
    V5  Hydrate, lazy join and bounds gate
        Generate A-to-B hydrate merges amid client predictions/writes/reads: higher/lower/equal
        versions, missing fields, unversioned contests, existing/absent answer references,
        hydrate stale cause, local frontiers, no prediction transfer, silent value baselines;
        hold C1-C8 and @plan.ServerRendering.O4's no-mismatch rule. Adopt overlapping domains
        mid-run: one entity object/id, one byKey and one registering domain, detached dormant
        decode, position-0 installation, single publication, rollback, unavailable loader and
        eager-loader warning. Test default 6 repair slots, fresh-page chains at N requests,
        failure/mid-chain invalidation, 64/65 per-record queue boundary and multi-record overflow
        with zero partial joins/predictions/work. Second-tab refusal starts no entity peer work.
        Planned; @plan.Testing.O6 owns host-helper replay and inconclusive results.
    V6  Evidence handoff and stage gate
        Retain references/evidence/README.md run commands and all model/mutant/type fixtures;
        port rather than ship the model. From references/evidence/type-spike run its documented
        positive and negative tsconfig checks with the pinned repository toolchain; from
        references/evidence run node runtime-model/model.mjs 20000 and mutant commands. Record
        observed counts instead of assuming historical README receipts. No edits to those files
        are required by this writing assignment. S5 is gated on @V1-@V3, S7 minimal Story support
        precedes the full property suite, and S6 entity persistence follows S5. Each stage uses
        @plan.Package.O4's packed-consumer gate; library implementation claims require executable
        proofs, not merely workflow check. Planned.
questions
    Q1  Measure repair concurrency and property-test workload
        Default repair concurrency 6 and initial 50-by-40 workload are design guesses, not open
        semantic choices. Recommended: measure S5 fixtures and Dispatch Desk page churn before
        changing them; retain conservative bounded repair and sample long interleavings.
    Q2  Field-narrowed targets and sampled production audit
        Recommended: defer both until measured demand. v1 targets retain @O1's forms and audit
        stays exhaustive, development/test only. This does not reopen adopted semantics.
